   "_dt": 1792700712.0,
   "ageDays": 0.78,
   "summary": "The bill, comprising 26 clauses, was transmitted to the state assembly by the National Assembly on October 6, 2026, for concurrence in line with Section 9(2) of the 1999 Constitution, as amended. He added that the report received the support of a two-thirds majority of members, in accordance with Order 17, Rule 3 of the Gombe State House of Assembly Standing Orders, 2020.",
   "summary_zh": "该法案共26条，已于2026年10月6日由国民议会提交州议会，以根据经修订的《1999年宪法》第9条第2款取得同意。他补充称，根据《2020年戈姆贝州议院常设议事规则》第17号令第3条，该报告获得了三分之二多数议员的支持。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The bill, comprising 26 clauses, was transmitted to the state assembly by the National Assembly on October 6, 2026, for concurrence in line with Section 9(2) of the 1999 Constitution, as amended. The proposed legislation seeks to amend the Constitution to establish a legal framework for a State Police Service, distinct from the Nigeria Police Force and under federal control. LEADERSHIP reports that the assembly passed the bill on Thursday after considering the report of its Ad-hoc Committee on the Review of the 1999 Constitution. Upon receipt of the bill, the House constituted a seven-member ad hoc committee, chaired by the Deputy Speaker, Hon. Sadam Bello Sale, to examine the proposed amendments. Presenting the committee’s report, Sale said members had consulted relevant stakeholders as part of their review of the bill. He added that the report received the support of a two-thirds majority of members, in accordance with Order 17, Rule 3 of the Gombe State House of Assembly Standing Orders, 2020. During Thursday’s plenary, lawmakers considered the report at the Committee of the Whole. They adopted recommendations to amend various provisions of the Constitution, including those relating to the establishment and operation of state police services separate from the federal police. The passage places Gombe among the states whose legislatures have endorsed the proposed constitutional changes to establish state police as part of efforts to address Nigeria’s security challenges. Commenting on the bill, the Speaker of the House, Abubakar Muhammad Luggerewo, said members unanimously supported the proposed legal framework for establishing state police, noting that it would strengthen security in the country. He commended the lawmakers for their commitment and resilience. Barr. Hauwa Abdullahi and Barr. Babadidi J. Mele served as secretaries to the committee. We’ve got the edge. Get real-time reports, breaking scoops, and exclusive angles delivered straight to your phone. Don’t settle for stale news. Join LEADERSHIP NEWS on WhatsApp for 24/7 updates → Join Our WhatsApp Channel --> --> Onuado Cynthia Cynthia Onuado is a journalist at Leadership Newspaper & TV with 10 years of experience in journalism reporting on politics, Foreign policies, social development, gender, and human interest stories across print and broadcast platforms. She is also a member of the Nigerian Institute Of Public Relations (NIPR). Cynthia’s work reflects hands-on newsroom experience and editorial diligence. She is committed to ethical, people-centred journalism that amplifies underrepresented voice. She can be reached at [email protected] or on X at @alwayscynthia0",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "ng"
   ],
