   "title": "CBN Takes Steps to Strengthen Nigeria-China Financial Corridor",
   "url": "https://www.thisdaylive.com/2026/10/22/cbn-takes-steps-to-strengthen-nigeria-china-financial-corridor/",
   "domain": "thisdaylive.com",
   "source": "ThisDay（尼日利亚）",
   "published": "Thu, 22 Oct 2026 17:11:49 +0000",
   "_dt": 1792689109.0,
   "ageDays": 0.91,
   "summary": "The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) has convened a high-level business and financial roundtable in China to explore opportunities to deepen Nigeria’s financial markets. The statement said that the engagement, with the theme, ‘Strengthening the Nigeria–China Financial Corridor,’ also aimed to identify practical barriers to stronger financial flows between the two economies.",
   "summary_zh": "尼日利亚中央银行（CBN）在中国召集了一场高级别商业与金融圆桌会议，探讨深化尼日利亚金融市场的机遇。声明称，此次活动以“加强尼日利亚—中国金融走廊”为主题，还旨在找出阻碍两国经济之间金融流动增强的实际障碍。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The Central Bank of Nigeria (CBN) has convened a high-level business and financial roundtable in China to explore opportunities to deepen Nigeria’s financial markets. According to a statement from the Corporate Communications Department of the CBN, the meeting held in Beijing on Wednesday, brought together Nigerian and Chinese business leaders. The statement said that the engagement, with the theme, ‘Strengthening the Nigeria–China Financial Corridor,’ also aimed to identify practical barriers to stronger financial flows between the two economies. “It provided a platform for businesses operating across both markets to share firsthand experiences and discuss how financial connectivity, banking relationships and investment channels could be strengthened,” it said. Meanwhile, the CBN Governor, Olayemi Cardoso, emphasised the importance of engaging directly with businesses to understand the opportunities and constraints affecting financial activity between Nigeria and China. Cardoso underscored the importance of translating stakeholder engagement into a clearer understanding of the financial and institutional issues requiring attention. He said particular attention would be paid to opportunities that could inform efforts to develop deeper, more accessible financial markets. Deputy Governor for Economic Policy at the CBN, Philip Ikeazor, in his opening remarks, set out the objectives of the engagement. Ikeazor said that it was important to translate stronger macroeconomic fundamentals into increased investment and more developed financial markets. The Nigerian Ambassador to the People’s Republic of China, Lt.-Gen. Abdulrahman Dambazau (rtd), also addressed participants. Other speakers included Chairperson of Blue Diamond Group, Festus Mbisiogu, and Chairperson of CIG Motors, Chief Diana Chen, who brought perspectives from businesses operating across the Nigeria–China corridor. At the heart of the engagement was an interactive session in which participants discussed opportunities for greater financial activity and the practical challenges encountered in conducting transactions between the two countries. Issues raised included the need for more efficient cross-border payment arrangements, stronger banking connectivity, and the potential benefits of a larger Nigerian banking presence in China. These were considered alongside broader opportunities to facilitate financial flows and to improve access to financial services and investment opportunities in Nigeria. The engagement also featured a presentation on Nigeria’s economic reform progress, improved macroeconomic fundamentals, and emerging investment opportunities in the country’s financial markets. The insights gathered are expected to inform the CBN’s continued engagement with relevant financial institutions and regulatory counterparts, focusing on practical areas for cooperation within its mandate. The Beijing roundtable is part of the CBN’s broader strategy to deepen Nigeria’s financial relationships across Asia. Following engagements in Singapore and at the IMF/World Bank Annual meetings in Bangkok, the apex bank’s China programme includes bilateral discussions with the People’s Bank of China. It also includes engagement with the leadership of the Industrial and Commercial Bank of China (ICBC), and meetings with Nigerian and Chinese businesses. The programme will also extend to Guangzhou, where the CBN will hold a listening session with the Nigerian business community. This is to better understand financial challenges and explore opportunities to strengthen remittance channels and facilitate investment in Nigeria. ( NAN ) Founded on January 22, 1995, THISDAY is published by THISDAY NEWSPAPERS LTD., 35 Creek Road Apapa, Lagos, Nigeria with offices in 36 states of Nigeria , the Federal Capital Territory and around the world. It is Nigeria’s most authoritative news media available on all platforms for the political, business, professional and diplomatic elite and broader middle classes while serving as the meeting point of new ideas, culture and technology for the aspirationals and millennials. The newspaper is a public trust dedicated to the pursuit of truth and reason covering a range of issues from breaking news to politics, business, the markets, the arts, sports and community to the crossroads of people and society. You can email us at: hello@thisdaylive.com or visit our contact us page",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
