   "title": "Share of irregular workers hits highest level since 2003 in Aug.: data",
   "url": "https://www.koreatimes.co.kr/economy/20261021/share-of-irregular-workers-hits-highest-level-since-2003-in-aug-data",
   "domain": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "source": "koreatimes.co.kr",
   "published": "Wed, 21 Oct 2026 14:32:04 GMT",
   "_dt": 1792593124.0,
   "ageDays": 2.02,
   "summary": "The proportion of irregular workers among South Korea's wage earners reached a record high of 38.5 percent in August, the data ministry said Wednesday, with their wage gap with regular workers also reaching a new record. The total number of irregular workers came to 8.67 million as of August, up from 8.56 million a year earlier, according to the Ministry of Data and Statistics.",
   "summary_zh": "韩国统计部周三表示，8月份韩国工资收入者中非正规就业者的比例达到创纪录的38.5%，他们与正规就业者的工资差距也创下新高。根据数据和统计部的数据，截至8月份，非正规就业者总人数达到867万人，较上年同期的856万人有所增加。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "The proportion of irregular workers among South Korea's wage earners reached a record high of 38.5 percent in August, the data ministry said Wednesday, with their wage gap with regular workers also reaching a new record. The total number of irregular workers came to 8.67 million as of August, up from 8.56 million a year earlier, according to the Ministry of Data and Statistics. South Korea conducts an annual survey on employment types among the country's economically active population based on figures from every August. The percentage of irregular salaried employees in the country's workforce was up from 38.2 percent tallied in 2025, the report showed. An official from the ministry said both the number and proportion of irregular workers reached their highest levels since South Korea began compiling related data under the current method in 2003. Male workers accounted for 3.7 million of the irregular workers, while the number of female workers came to 4.96 million. By sector, 21.5 percent of irregular workers were employed in the health and social welfare sector, followed by the accommodation and food service industry with 9.9 percent. As of August, the total number of wage workers came to 22.52 million, with those holding regular positions reaching 13.85 million, or 61.5 percent, of the total. The report also showed that the average weekly working hours for irregular workers came to 29.5 hours, up 1.3 hours from a year earlier. The figure for regular workers came to 40.4 hours, compared with 36.5 hours a year earlier. The average monthly salary of irregular workers over the past three months came to 2.11 million won ($1,590), up 24,000 won from a year earlier. In comparison, the monthly wage of regular workers came to 4.05 million won, up 156,000 won over the period. The wage gap between the two groups widened by 132,000 won to 1.94 million won from the previous year, the report showed. Their average length of service came to three years, up one month from a year earlier. The data ministry said 36.5 percent of irregular workers were enrolled in the national pension program, down 0.6 percentage point from a year earlier. It showed that 53 percent of them were covered by the public health insurance program, down 0.2 percentage point over the period. By comparison, 88.6 percent and 95.7 percent of regular workers were enrolled in the national pension and public health insurance programs, respectively. According to the Organization for Economic Cooperation and Development's (OECD) data, temporary workers accounted for 27.5 percent of the total laborers in South Korea in 2025, compared with 5.1 percent in Britain and 14.2 percent in Japan. The OECD calculation only considers wage earners and salary workers whose job has a pre-determined termination date as temporary workers and does not put part-timers, outsourced laborers and those who renew their employment contracts in the category",
   "bodyLevel": "fulltext",
   "country": [
    "kr"
   ],
