   "summary": "Ten House lawmakers in the Philippines filed House Resolution No. 1562 urging President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. to declare Chinese Ambassador Jing Quan persona non grata or request his recall from Beijing. MANILA, Philippines – Ten House lawmakers filed a proposed resolution urging President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. to either declare Chinese Ambassador Jing Quan persona non grata or ask Beijing to recall him.",
   "summary_zh": "菲律宾10名众议员提交了第1562号众议院决议，敦促总统小费迪南德·马科斯宣布中国大使景泉为不受欢迎的人或要求北京召回他。马尼拉，菲律宾——10名众议员提交了一项拟议决议，敦促总统小费迪南德·马科斯要么宣布中国大使景泉为不受欢迎的人，要么要求北京召回他。",
   "summarySource": "fulltext",
   "body": "Already have Rappler+? Sign in to listen to groundbreaking journalism. Ten House lawmakers in the Philippines filed House Resolution No. 1562 urging President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. to declare Chinese Ambassador Jing Quan persona non grata or request his recall from Beijing. The resolution was prompted by Jing's controversial statement perceived as a threat to the Philippines, alongside the Chinese Embassy's dismissive response to the lawmakers' concerns. The lawmakers emphasized the need for decisive action to protect national sovereignty and criticized the Chinese Embassy's language as undiplomatic and arrogant. This is AI-generated. Read the article for full context. Report any errors . MANILA, Philippines – Ten House lawmakers filed a proposed resolution urging President Ferdinand Marcos Jr. to either declare Chinese Ambassador Jing Quan persona non grata or ask Beijing to recall him. House Senior Deputy Minority Leader Leila de Lima (Mamamayang Liberal) and House Assistant Minority Leader Chel Diokno (Akbayan) joined eight other lawmakers in authoring House Resolution No. 1562, filed on Wednesday, October 21. Their move followed similar resolutions filed in the upper chamber by senators Erwin Tulfo and Risa Hontiveros. The lawmakers cite a number of incidents that led to the filing of the resolution, starting with Jing’s October 15 statement saying that if China wants to use force, “we may easily win.” Philippine government officials, including the President, later said the statement sounded like a threat. The Chinese Embassy dared the Senate and the House to just declare Jing as persona non grata and “stop bluffing.” It also threatened to block Philippine officials from entering China if Jing were to be declared persona non grata. Before the group of House lawmakers filed the resolution, they sounded the alarm over Jing’s seeming threat against the Philippines, and the Chinese embassy’s subsequent language that seemed dismissive of Filipinos’ sentiments. “The embassy’s undiplomatic language taunting our government to declare the Chinese ambassador as persona non grata does little to encourage serious dialogue between two sovereign nations. Instead it reflects their position that our country should simply accept China’s threats and submit to its demands,” they said. “The Philippines must not be cowed into silence in the face of such arrogance,” the lawmakers added. – Rappler.com Upgrade to Rappler+ for exclusive content and unlimited access",
